the first thing I'd like to say is you  need to rely on those skills that you  already have as a parent many of the  skills that we have as teachers require  the same kinds of skills that we use as  parents working with our children  so rely on those skills that you already  have as a parents to lead and to guide  your children in their understanding  another thing I would say to you is  working with your children at home  provides a wonderful opportunity to  provide in an educative experience not  just in what they know and understand  and what they'll hear the knowledge that  will acquire but you also have a  wonderful opportunity to form their  character with children working at home  they're working on these academic skills  they're acquiring a character virtues as  well one that jumps to my mind  is is that the virtue of temperance or  self-control or self-discipline working  at home oftentimes requires those kinds  of things so it really is an opportunity  for children to continue their education  and to develop the skills that they're  learning at school the last thing I'll  say just in general is to relax you can  do this you're not going to ruin your  child in terms of education and in in  terms of schooling just relax take a  long term view of their education  schooling is one part of their education  but it's not everything when it comes to  their education so so do your best keep  it simple provide for them what you can  and help them out today I'm just going  to give you a few tips recommendations  for helping your your child or your  children to learn at home  that will make the time more productive  more rewarding for you but especially  for your children so let's get started I  have my tips here up on the board that  should offer a little bit of advice for  you the first thing I'd like to point  out is location choose a location in  your home that will allow your child to  get the most out of their experience  what this typically means is that you  need to identify a location in your home  that's well-lit a place similar to you  know the desk that they might use at  school so a kitchen table a dining room  table a desk in a room would be would be  a good idea  make sure it's well-lit as I said but  more importantly and most importantly is  make sure it's free of any kind of a  distraction now when when we're thinking  about describe any kind of a distraction  we often think about things like the  television being on or videos on a  computer playing or some kind of music  playing in the background or something  like that  and that's absolutely the case be  mindful of that and be careful that you  haven't created your your a study  location in a place that's that's are  distracting in those ways but also be  careful about conversations  conversations that you have with your  other children conversation between  between our spouses or telephone  conversations those can be really  distracting as well so create that space  created one that's free about any kind  of a distraction so that students can  concentrate on the work that they have  before them the next thing is to create  a schedule it's a very good idea to  schedule out the school day that would  be similar to what a child would  experience at school make sure that you  line things  with with that times if students I get  through a particular subject at a  particular time allow them to move on to  to the next thing again this will teach  them you know the value of working hard  and accomplishing you know their tasks  so that they can move on to other things  now in creating a schedule I highly  recommend that you start with the  subjects that are most challenging for  your child oftentimes early in the  morning after waking up and having a  breakfast and having a little bit of  time to wake up  I'm children are often fresh in the  morning and they're able to tackle those  things that are most challenging the  longer you wait in the day as you wait a  little bit longer their energy wears out  they get tired and so again tackle those  those subjects those areas that are most  challenging for that particular child it  could be math it could be reading it  could be language could be any of those  just make sure that you try to tackle  those things at the beginning another  thing that I want to point out in terms  of a schedule is make sure that you  provide breaks for your child even high  school students need breaks I would  recommend at least a 5 to 10 minutes of  a break per hour and that goes all the  way up to high school and as you go  lower I'm you're gonna want to do more  breaks and more frequent breaks the idea  is this if children are given the time  to step back and just and to stand up  and to step away from their work and  rejuvenate when they come come back to  their work they'll be more refreshed and  able to tackle everything that they need  to tackle and what you'll discover is if  you give those breaks periodically even  if they say I'm not ready for a break  have them stand up stretch whatever  because when they get back to their work  they'll they'll be much more able to  able to focus on what is what it is they  have to do  after that item number three is  questions ask questions ask lots of  questions throughout the day when you're  at lunch when you're at dinnertime when  you're taking a break ask a question  even if you don't know the answer ask  them - to talk about what was the most  interesting thing that you read in your  history last hour um what was the most  important thing that you think and don't  be afraid to say if if you ask a  question and your child says I don't  know don't be afraid to say ah I don't  know either  be willing to say let's go and let's all  find out together what that answer is or  if you don't have time to go and look up  the answer ask your child to - go and I  find the answer and then come back and  tell you what they think it is number  four on my list here is siblings it's a  good idea to get older siblings involved  if there are older siblings in the  household call on them ask them invite  them to participate in the education of  a particular child as long as those old  older siblings are taking care of their  own work you can invite them in ask them  to help my younger sibling it's a good  idea for a lot of reasons if you're  trying to explain something to your  child and you know you're not able to do  it well maybe another child can come in  and look at it through more childlike  eyes and try to explain it to a younger  a younger member of the family all right  the next item on the list is resources  explore other resources beyond the  curriculum that is provided by the  school there's a lot of things out there  online and of course you need to be  careful as you look at those you need to  look at those and review them before  your child is able to see them  no but explore those and you  use those to great effect use the  program that your school has provided  whether that's an online platform or not  use those to the extent that the school  is asking you to use those but don't be  afraid to go beyond those one caution is  that this is not the time to learn  something complicated online make sure  that if you're going to use it with your  children that you have a relatively good  understanding about how that resource  works and that you can use it  proficiently because if in doubt if you  don't know it really well I would highly  recommend that you put it off to the  side because there there are very few  things that are as good as a good book  in a paper and in a pencil so you know  don't be distracted don't be enticed you  know by a particular program that you  don't know very well and that it's  actually gonna take you time to learn  and it's going to take you time to  explain to your child use what you  already use to the greatest effect and  last but not least is read  if in doubt read read read read if  you're not comfortable with any other  you know kinds of technology and you  want to supplement the firt your first  option should be a good book look for  books make books available to your  children provide them with access to  great books depending on the age you  want your child to be reading aloud in  fact this is a great way to get actually  family members involved with one another  in terms of the education is have a  younger child read aloud to an older  child and read aloud to your own  children do it in pairs do it as a small  group or do it as an entire family  reading aloud is  a lost art and I would love to see that  recaptured and in one of the places  where we can see this happening again in  America is in the home so read aloud  make I'm reading a part a large part of  the academic day in conclusion I'll just  say this you're going to be able to do a  good job just trusting your instincts  rely on your common sense most likely  the skills that you've already acquired  as a parent are the skills that you're  going to need in order to provide a  quality education for your children
 

